WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.030
There are good boys,

00:00:02.030 --> 00:00:04.940
and then there are very good boys

00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:08.230
like these dogs here.
They're searching for a scent

00:00:08.230 --> 00:00:10.600
that no human can detect:

00:00:10.600 --> 00:00:12.733
the scent of an epileptic seizure.

00:00:17.470 --> 00:00:21.140
We've long known that dogs
can detect seizures in humans

00:00:21.140 --> 00:00:24.840
in some cases 45 minutes
before they occur.

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:28.010
That's one reason why
organizations like Handi'chiens

00:00:28.010 --> 00:00:31.453
in France provide service
dogs for people with epilepsy.

00:00:56.650 --> 00:01:00.070
And in some cases, this
can prove lifesaving.

00:01:00.070 --> 00:01:02.740
It might give people
time to take medication

00:01:02.740 --> 00:01:06.090
that could prevent or reduce
the severity of a seizure

00:01:06.090 --> 00:01:08.900
or move somewhere safer where an injury

00:01:08.900 --> 00:01:10.870
is less likely to occur.

00:01:10.870 --> 00:01:11.703
Incredible?

00:01:11.703 --> 00:01:12.536
Yes.

00:01:12.536 --> 00:01:13.855
But proven?

00:01:13.855 --> 00:01:17.590
Not until French researchers
teamed up with Medical Mutts,

00:01:17.590 --> 00:01:21.963
a US-based organization that
trains seizure alert dogs.

00:01:31.080 --> 00:01:33.271
That marker, they believed, was a scent

00:01:33.271 --> 00:01:34.987
that dogs can detect.

00:01:35.180 --> 00:01:38.630
So in 2018, they set up an experiment.

00:01:38.630 --> 00:01:41.290
First, they collected dozen of samples of

00:01:41.290 --> 00:01:43.260
breath and sweat from people with

00:01:43.260 --> 00:01:45.230
different forms of epilepsy.

00:01:45.230 --> 00:01:48.460
Some of them were taken during
or right after a seizure,

00:01:48.460 --> 00:01:52.660
while others were collected
after exercise or at rest.

00:01:52.660 --> 00:01:55.340
Then they distributed
them among seven different

00:01:55.340 --> 00:01:57.740
steel containers in this room.

00:01:57.740 --> 00:02:01.940
Finally, they let out,
or, they let in the dogs.

00:02:01.940 --> 00:02:05.580
One by one, Casey, Dodger,
Lana, Zoey, and Roo

00:02:05.580 --> 00:02:06.910
walked into the room.

00:02:06.910 --> 00:02:09.350
They were trained to stop and stand still

00:02:09.350 --> 00:02:12.020
if they think they detected
the scent of a seizure.

00:02:12.020 --> 00:02:14.400
And if they were right, they got a treat,

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:15.360
good dog!

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:18.940
To the researchers' excitement,
the canines excelled.

00:02:18.940 --> 00:02:21.490
Three of the dogs,
Casey, Dodger, and Zoey,

00:02:21.490 --> 00:02:24.350
sniffed out the odor
associated with seizure with

00:02:24.350 --> 00:02:26.630
100% accuracy.

00:02:26.630 --> 00:02:29.970
The two other pups, Lana
and Roo, who had less

00:02:29.970 --> 00:02:32.215
time to train, weren't quite as accurate.

00:02:32.215 --> 00:02:35.360
But they still correctly
identified two-thirds

00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:37.790
of the seizure samples on their first try.

00:02:37.790 --> 00:02:40.250
What makes these results
even more remarkable

00:02:40.250 --> 00:02:43.420
is that the scent samples
were from different people

00:02:43.420 --> 00:02:47.073
and also produced by
different kinds of seizures.

00:02:56.027 --> 00:03:00.050
And what exactly is that marker made of?

00:03:00.050 --> 00:03:02.620
Here's the thing: We still don't know.

00:03:02.620 --> 00:03:05.650
It's likely that seizures
trigger a change in the body's

00:03:05.650 --> 00:03:08.380
electrical activity, the researchers say.

00:03:08.380 --> 00:03:10.030
And those changes can affect the

00:03:10.030 --> 00:03:12.340
composition of odor molecules that

00:03:12.340 --> 00:03:15.980
we emit through our sweat,
breath, and, likely, urine.

00:03:15.980 --> 00:03:18.380
Now, whether people emit these odors

00:03:18.380 --> 00:03:22.210
before a seizure in time
to reduce its worst effects

00:03:22.210 --> 00:03:23.420
is still in question,

00:03:23.420 --> 00:03:26.810
and it's not something that
the researchers tested.

00:03:26.810 --> 00:03:30.060
But some experts claim
that people emit a specific

00:03:30.060 --> 00:03:34.900
group of odor chemicals 15 to
45 minutes prior to seizing,

00:03:34.900 --> 00:03:37.250
which dogs can detect.

00:03:37.250 --> 00:03:41.970
So what exactly makes canines
such smell superstars?

00:03:41.970 --> 00:03:44.560
It's their incredible noses.

00:03:44.560 --> 00:03:48.980
With as many as 300 million
olfactory receptors,

00:03:48.980 --> 00:03:52.670
a dog's nose is up to 100 thousand times

00:03:52.670 --> 00:03:53.963
stronger than our own.

00:03:55.180 --> 00:03:57.210
That means they can detect
a few scent molecules

00:03:57.210 --> 00:03:59.420
among trillions of them.

00:03:59.420 --> 00:04:02.750
Scientists are now trying
to build electronic noses

00:04:02.750 --> 00:04:04.670
that are just as powerful.

00:04:04.670 --> 00:04:06.270
The idea is that they too

00:04:06.270 --> 00:04:08.700
could be used to sniff out diseases.

00:04:08.700 --> 00:04:13.300
But for now e-noses are
nowhere near as good as dogs,

00:04:13.300 --> 00:04:17.303
and in some ways, doesn't
that seem like a good thing?

